  The weapon an ambitious wizard should be using is the player's brain.
Wizards can rarely rely on just one item in the game.  When they do rely
on just one item, it should be wielded +6 or +7 Magicbane.  (Note: Some
posters disagree with me here.)

>> Stormbringer or one of the Brands ...
>
>Stormy rules but a wizard should view melee' as a backup
>to remote combat approaches.  Avoid letting stuff get into
>your face when possible.

  I agree with Doug about using ranged combat:  Melee combat is one of
the best ways to die in NetHack.  If the monster is in melee range, so
are you.

  I use Magicbane in preference to Stormbringer.  That's due to other
advantages wielded Magicbane gives, and the disadvantage that wielding
Stormbringer gives.  (Stormbringer I have carried to the Astral plane,
where I only wielded it long enough to let me tame Pestilence and
Famine.)  I also use +7 Magicbane in preference to +7 Grayswandir.

>
>> You might #name Orcrist and Sting (but, for Anhur's sake, don't
>> *use* the pieces of junk)...
>
>I disagree about Sting.  For any class that can get to
>Expert in dagger it can be worth wielding it until getting
>Magicbane or other better melee weapon and throwing
>it is fine.

  Like so many items/tricks/techniques, Sting's relative worth varies
greatly over the course of the game.  It's largest effect will probably
being during the first 10k or so turns in a game.   So at best Sting is
a stepping stone to better things, like a character still alive after
10k turns. 

  Even so, note that a starting wizard shouldn't just "wield and forget"
Sting on DL 1 at XL 1.  Sting and the starting quarterstaff should be
used when and as appropriate for a) keeping alive and b) increasing
dagger skill.

  A +1 quarterstaff at basic skill does 2 to 7 points of damage per hit,
with a +0 to-hit.  Unskilled +0 Sting against a non-orc small monster
does -1 to 3 points of damage, with a -2 to-hit.  So only wield Sting
against safe monsters until you're at basic dagger skill.  Don't abandon
the quarterstaff until you're "skilled" with daggers.  (Note that
throwing daggers looks like the safest way to increase dagger skill.)

  Pretty complicated, huh?  This is one of the many interesting aspects
of playing a wizard.  Just one because proper spell/mana usage and spell
skills advancement is infinitely more complex.  (Compare to the deep and
complex game strategy of "Valk SMASH!")


